<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François Quesnay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1694">1694</a>–<a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François Quesnay was a French economist and physician of the Physiocratic school, born at Méré near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>. Apprenticed to a surgeon and qualified in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, he became physician in ordinary to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> and was installed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles</a>, where the king called him his thinker. Around 1750 he and Jacques C.M.V. de Gournay became the centre of the group later known as the Physiocrates, which included the elder Mirabeau and du Pont de Nemours. His Tableau économique of 1758, perhaps the first work to describe the workings of the economy analytically, provided the foundations of their ideas, and his Le Despotisme de la Chine of 1767 supported enlightened despotism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-quesnay.jpg" alt="François Quesnay" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Portrait_du_docteur_François_Quesnay_(1694-1774),_médecin_et_économiste_-_P683_-_Musée_Carnavalet_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
